WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.270
YUFENG GUO: Pandas are
majestic eaters of bamboo,

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.570
and very good at sleeping
for long periods of time.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:10.560
But they also have
a secret power--

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.960
chomping down on
large data sets.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.320
Today we'll introduce one of the
most powerful and popular tools

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.290
in data wrangling, and
it's called Pandas.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.300
When you think of
data science, pandas

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.460
is probably not the first
thing that comes to mind.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.626
These black and white bears
spend most of their time

00:00:25.626 --> 00:00:28.830
eating bamboo and sleeping,
not doing data science.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.369
But today, we'll use Pandas
to manipulate our data sets

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.900
and set it up for
machine learning.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.560
I can't do the entire library
justice in just one video,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.500
but hopefully this overview
will help you get going.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.050
And I'll leave it to you to
explore the fascinating world

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.570
of Pandas in more depth.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.000
Pandas is an open
source Python library

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.360
that provides easy to use, high
performance data structures

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.310
and data analysis tools.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.765
Cuddly bears aside, the name
comes from the term panel data,

00:00:57.765 --> 00:01:00.015
a common term for
multi-dimensional data

00:01:00.015 --> 00:01:03.750
sets, encountered in both
statistics and econometrics.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.800
To install Pandas, simply run
$ pip install pandas inside

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.570
your Python environment.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.530
Then we can import pandas as pd.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.720
One of the most common
things that Pandas ends up

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.750
being used for is
reading in CSV files

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.240
using the
[? read csv ?] function.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.750
It is often the starting
point for using Pandas.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.060
Pandas loads this
data into a DataFrame.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.090
This can be thought of
as essentially a table

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.556
or a spreadsheet.

00:01:31.556 --> 00:01:33.180
We can take a quick
glimpse of our data

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.750
by calling head on our
DataFrame DataFrames

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.630
have rows of data with
named columns, which

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.390
in Pandas are called series.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.060
For me, one of the nicest
things about DataFrames

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.200
is the describe function, which
displays a table of statistics

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.580
about your DataFrame.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.690
This is super useful for
sanity checking your data set,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.430
seeing if the distribution of
your data looks reasonable,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.200
and whether the properties are
what you expect them to be.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.567
I also sometimes will use
Pandas to shuffle my data.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.150
This can be useful
in situations where

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.520
you want to shuffle
the entire data set,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.229
rather than merely
having a look ahead

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.480
buffer as you extract it out.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.510
For example, if your data
is not shuffled at all,

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.950
and it is, in fact,
already sorted,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.250
you'd want to give it
an extra good mixing.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.830
However, for truly
huge data sets,

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.440
it's possible that this may
be impractical without more

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.350
sophisticated approaches.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.110
To access a particular
column of a data set,

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:33.410
use bracket notation to pull
out that column, passing

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.109
in the name of that column.

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:37.400
If you're wondering what the
possible column names are,

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.340
you can look back at
the output of .describe,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.200
or use df or dataframe.columns
to access all the columns

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.660
in the DataFrame as an array.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.295
If you can access
data column by column,

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:51.920
then the next thing
you'll want to know

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.500
is how to access it by row.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.750
Accessing rows of
DataFrames is a little bit

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.310
different than from columns.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.420
For example, if we want the
row with an index i of a given

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.690
DataFrame, we can use iloc,
or i location, index location,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.430
and i.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.340
Keep in mind that Pandora
operates on a 0 based indexing

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:10.920
system.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.000
So the first row is
actually index 0.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.420
Sometimes you may want a
particular row and column.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.120
Because rows and columns
are accessed differently,

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.970
we'll need different
techniques to get that done.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.930
In particular, if you want
row 5 of a column called name,

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.300
you would use
something like this.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.640
And where things get
much more interesting

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.450
is when you want to get a whole
range of columns and rows.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.510
On the column side, the
way to get multiple columns

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:42.335
is to pass in an array
of those column names.

00:03:42.335 --> 00:03:44.960
If there are many, many columns
and you don't want to type them

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.820
all out, you can use the columns
array, the .columns array,

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.970
and select a range of
column names from there,

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.330
and use that to select columns.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.100
If we want to get
a range of rows,

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.980
we can use colon notation
inside the brackets

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.680
that follow iloc, like so.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.800
And the starting index here
is included, while the ending

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.610
index is excluded.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.860
So this will return row
indices 5, 6, 7, 8, and 9,

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.470
but not row index 10.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:16.860
Say we wanted both--

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.810
both a subset of columns
and a subset of rows.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.380
What would that look like?

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.470
Well, we can combine
what we've learned so far

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:27.470
and make one expression
that gets the job done.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.130
So first we'll choose the column
names, just like we did before.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.490
Then we get the columns
back, and then we

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.360
can select the rows from that
DataFrame using that colon

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:39.710
notation.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.480
And so once you get
the hang of things,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.940
you'll actually be able to
collapse these three lines down

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.440
into just one expression.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.060
Something like this.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.560
And if this is the
first time you're

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.870
seeing this sort
of collapsing, I'd

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.120
encourage you to
pause here and look

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.030
at how this expression is
exactly equivalent to what

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.640
we showed earlier.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.460
I'll still be here
when you get back.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.730
So happy to wait.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.910
We've looked at some simple
data manipulation in this video,

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:08.120
and there is much more that
the Pandas ecosystem offers,

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.060
from its efficient file storage
in the form of pie tables

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.710
and HDF5 format to running
certain statistical analyzes.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.890
Now this has been just a quick
overview of what Pandas can do,

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:20.990
so check out the
documentation links below

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.870
to start your
journey with Pandas.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.010
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.680
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.900
to check out future episodes
